   {
    "id": "t38_intro",
    "type": "flograph.util.note",
    "pos": [
     0,
     0
    ],
    "params": {
     "text": "# Machine learning, step by step\n\nThis flow is a worked tutorial. Read the notes **top to bottom**: each band is\none chapter, with the explanation on the left and the nodes doing it on the\nright. Press **Run All** (F5), then change things and watch what moves.\n\n**The question.** 160 house sales. Can we predict a house's **price** from\nits size, rooms, age, garden and city (*regression*)? Its price **band**\n(*classification*)? And do the houses fall into natural **groups** nobody\nlabelled (*clustering*)?\n\n**The shape every ML node wants: a tidy table.** One **row per example**\n(one house), one **column per measurement** (a *feature*), and — for\nregression and classification — one column holding the answer you want\npredicted (the *target*). Everything here is a pandas **DataFrame** flowing\ndown a wire; a fitted encoder, scaler or model flows down a wire too, as an\n**object** you plug into the next node.\n\n| Your data | Good fit? | What to do first |\n|---|---|---|\n| numbers (size, age, spend) | yes — the native food | maybe **Feature Scaler** |\n| a few categories (city, plan) | yes | **One-Hot Encode** |\n| ordered or target categories | yes | **Label Encode** |\n| yes / no columns | yes | nothing — read as 0 / 1 |\n| dates | after a step | **Date Part** / **Date Arithmetic** → numbers |\n| a time series | after a step | lags with **Window Function**; split *without* shuffle |\n| gaps (empty cells) | no — refused | **Missing Values** first |\n| free text, images | no — not these nodes | LLM nodes; an image pack |\n\n**How big?** Everything happens in memory on this PC: from a few dozen rows\n(less is guessing) to a few hundred thousand is comfortable.",
     "width": 1440,
     "height": 560
    },
    "code": null,
    "label": null
   },
   {
    "id": "t38_n1",
    "type": "flograph.util.note",
    "pos": [
     0,
     800
    ],
    "params": {
     "text": "## 1 · Know your data\n\n**Houses** is a Table node — type over any cell and everything after it\nre-runs.\n\n**Correlation Matrix** asks which numbers move together. `price_k` against\n`size_m2` is near +1: size will be the strongest clue. Hover a cell for the\npair. Two *features* that are almost +1 with each other say the same thing\ntwice — keep one. It needs **number** columns; text is skipped.\n\n**Outlier Detection** finds rows that don't fit. Row 38 has a price of 4,200\n(someone typed an extra zero). One row like that drags a regression line\ntowards itself and gives K-Means a cluster of one. *IQR* (the default) is\nrobust because the outlier can't stretch the scale it is judged on.\n\nLook at it first (*flag only*, top), then drop it (*drop*, bottom): the\ncleaned table is published as **houses** with a Goto, and every later\nchapter picks it up with a From — no long wires.",
     "width": 620,
     "height": 620
    },
    "code": null,
    "label": null
   },
