   {
    "id": "t38_n2",
    "type": "flograph.util.note",
    "pos": [
     0,
     1880
    ],
    "params": {
     "text": "## 2 · Prepare the features\n\n**Train/Test Split** holds back a quarter of the rows (**test**) that the\nmodel never sees while it learns (**train**). Scoring a model on rows it\nlearned from is like marking your own homework. *Stratify by* `band` keeps\neach band's share the same on both sides. For anything in time order,\nuntick *Shuffle* so you test on the most recent rows.\n\n**One-Hot Encode** turns `city` into 0/1 columns (`city_Leeds`,\n`city_York`). Use it for categories with **no order** and not too many\nvalues. *Drop first* leaves one out (Hull = both 0), which linear models\nneed.\n\n**Label Encode** (bottom) turns categories into 0, 1, 2… Use it for a\n**target** or a category with a real order, and it can *decode* codes back\nto words. Don't feed codes for an unordered category to a linear model —\nit would read York (2) as \"twice\" Leeds (1).\n\n**Feature Scaler** puts the numbers on one scale. Linear, ridge, lasso,\nlogistic and K-Means need it; tree models don't care.\n\n**The golden rule** — look at the wires crossing between the rows: the\n**test** side doesn't learn its own encoder or scaler, it is handed the\n**train** side's. Fit on train, *apply* to test, or test-row information\nleaks into the model.",
     "width": 620,
     "height": 760
    },
    "code": null,
    "label": null
   },
